// Returning owning references in a value tuple hands them to the caller, so
// none of them is released on the way out.
//
// A function returning one reference names it, and flowScopeDealias exempts
// that one name from the scope's release list. A multi-value return hands back
// a value tuple instead, which is not a name -- so every element matched
// nothing, and all of them were released. The caller then released them again:
// a use after free followed by a double free, in a program that compiled clean.
//
// The count is the whole claim and no run can see it. Both allocations here are
// handed to the caller, so a correct 'pair' adjusts no counter at all -- which
// is why this file holds nothing else, and why the check in cases.toml can
// exclude the counter adjustment across the whole module rather than having to
// name a register.
//
// The caller's side of this is not asserted here. Destructuring a multi-value
// return does no move-or-copy accounting yet, which is a question about where a
// destructuring's alias adjustment attaches; workitems/Copy & Alias vs. Move
// Semantics carries it.

fn pair() +rc-mut i32, +rc-mut i32 {
  imm a = +rc-mut 1i32
  imm b = +rc-mut 2i32
  a, b
}
